  "question": {
    "text": "The function $f:R \\sim\\{0\\} \\rightarrow R$ given by\n$f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{e^{2 x}-1}$\ncan be made continuous at $x=0$ by defining $f(0)$ as",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "kF6oC",
      "text": "\n$2$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "a69cC",
      "text": "\n$-1$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5qjZN",
      "text": "\n$0$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "yS20Z",
      "text": "\n$1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{e^{2 x}-1}$\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{e^{2 x}-1-2 x}{x\\left(e^{2 x}-1\\right)}$\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{2 e^{2 x}-2}{\\left(e^{2 x}-1\\right)+2 x e^{2 x}}$\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{4 e^{2 x}}{4 e^{2 x}+4 x e^{2 x}}=1$.",
    "image": null
  },
